 favorite, though the state's Republican-leaning electorate and the age of the latest polling keep this short of safe.", "watch": "Fresh fall polling or a national environment shifting toward Republicans could tighten the race if Biggs consolidates the party base.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's governor race pits incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs against Republican Rep. Andy Biggs, whom she leads by an average of about eight points in surveys conducted since July. Those polls include ones sponsored by Republican-leaning firms, and independent pollsters have shown her ahead by double digits. Hobbs has also raised roughly three times as much money as Biggs, giving her a clear advertising edge.\n\nBiggs, a former House Freedom Caucus chairman, is a polarizing figure statewide, and recent coverage has focused on his governing record and the candidates' dueling attack ads. Most rating outlets call the race Lean Democrat, with one at Likely Democrat, and prediction markets price her chances near ninety percent. Arizona's slight Republican lean and the age of the latest polling leave room for movement, but the evidence points to Hobbs as a clear favorite entering the fall.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 7.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8968, "margin": 6.8}]}